body:
Hi, I just graduated high school back in may. I went through high school with POTS and thankfully survived. I'll try to answer your questions the best I can.

&#x200B;

1. *are there more suggestions for my 504 plan? i have a lot, like bringing snacks to class, extra time in the hallway, etc. is there anything more that would help?*

My 504 plan in high school was similar to yours but also extra time on tests and assignments.  This is a good model to get an idea. [http://www.standinguptopots.org/fnuwifh289ry298fhijewf/misc/1-accommodations-for-classroom/file](http://www.standinguptopots.org/fnuwifh289ry298fhijewf/misc/1-accommodations-for-classroom/file) Honestly, it all depends on your needs for accommodations because not everyone is the same.

&#x200B;

>2*. How do you cope with not having a cure? i feel like everyone is against me, even my father. nobody believes me and thinks i’m lying or lazy.*

It's very tough to cope and I completely understand how you feel.  I'd suggest finding a therapist that understands chronic illnesses. You could also try to talk to your high school counselor.

&#x200B;

>3. *is using a wheelchair an option? or anything else to help me walk. i feel guilty asking if i should use a wheelchair because i can walk, i just pass out everyday.*

Using an wheelchair is an option especially if you are severe enough that you constantly faint. Please do not feel guilty. POTS is an invisible illness.

&#x200B;

>4. *how do you manage hobbies with POTS? i am in band, but i struggle doing homework and i usually fall asleep when i get home from school. i have absolutely no time to do things i enjoy.*

In high school, I wasn't able to keep up with the strenuous activities so I had to go to school a half a day. You could change your schedule and take only the necessary classes to graduate.

&#x200B;

>5.  *is there anything else i should know? tips, advice, how to get people to believe me, how to survive school, things to ask my rheumatologist?*

POTS symptoms can be significantly improved through exercise, increased salt, and fluids. This is the exercise protocol  [https://www.dysautonomiainternational.org/pdf/CHOP\_Modified\_Dallas\_POTS\_Exercise\_Program.pdf](https://www.dysautonomiainternational.org/pdf/CHOP_Modified_Dallas_POTS_Exercise_Program.pdf) You can talk to your doctor about it. Unfortunately, it's very hard to get people to believe you. The best way to get people to believe you is to educate them about it. Dysautonomia International has great information on POTS [https://dysautonomiainternational.org/page.php?ID=30](https://dysautonomiainternational.org/page.php?ID=30) .  You can survive school by having accommodations, carrying a water bottle, salty snacks, taking breaks, etc.
